// XFAIL: DF-225b an UNDEFINED TYPE NAME in a signature is never diagnosed as
// one. It becomes an opaque type the checker carries silently, so what the
// reader gets is either a downstream mismatch about a type that does not exist
// (``cannot assign `Float` to variable of type `Float64` ``) or — when the
// signature reaches codegen, as here — `internal compiler error: Undefined
// struct: Float64`, with no location at all.
//
// `Float64` is the motivating spelling (DF-225c, ruled Aug 20: FLOAT ONLY —
// `Float` is the float type and `Float64` names nothing). Design 240 item 4
// went looking for the `Float64` type-name registration the ruling asked to
// remove and found there is none: `Float64` behaves exactly as `Nonesuch`
// does, which is the finding. So the compiler half of that ruling is not a
// removal — it is this diagnostic, and this diagnostic is DF-225b's class,
// not one name's.
//
// POSITIONS (probed Aug 21): an annotated `let` and a struct FIELD produce the
// silent-opaque face; a free FUNCTION SIGNATURE that reaches codegen produces
// the ICE — a third position beside DF-225b's original two (an enum case
// payload, a `sizeof<>` type argument).
//
// The assertion is deliberately not a wording: a clean, LOCATED diagnostic
// that names the type, and no internal compiler error.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: Float64
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: internal compiler error

func idn(x: Float64) -> Float64 { x }

func main() {
    print("unreachable")
}
